<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Cleartext is permitted, and the restriction lives one layer up.

    Alma's direct listener speaks plain ws:// and has no TLS port, so reaching a
    desktop over the LAN means unencrypted transport. This file is the system
    gate that decides whether the WebView may open such a socket at all.

    It cannot express the rule this app actually wants. <domain> matches a host
    name or a literal IP, never a subnet, so "only addresses on the phone's own
    network" is not writable here. What does express it is the app: a native
    plugin reads the phone's real subnets from ConnectivityManager and a direct
    address is only dialled when it shares one of them (see client/lan.ts,
    LocalNetworkPlugin.java and the allowDirect gate in client/transport.ts).

    So the split is deliberate:
      - this file decides that plain text is possible at all;
      - the app decides which addresses may use it.

    Everything past that — the payload — is sealed end to end regardless, and
    the desktop must still prove it holds the pairing code before a single
    request is trusted.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
